MCAS Cutaneous Predominant
Complex Fatigue Entities
Dermatological histamine hypersensitivity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-027
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of rapid-onset, widespread urticaria and severe flushing to rule out anaphylaxis and airway compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden, explosive development of full-body hives, severe cutaneous burning, and panic. Immediate on-site assessment was required to definitively exclude evolving anaphylactic shock, check airway patency, and administer acute parenteral antihistamines to halt systemic degranulation prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My skin is literally burning off, I am covered in giant red welts, and I can't stop scratching.
History of Present Illness: Patient with suspected mast cell activation reports a severe acute dermatological flare starting 2 hours ago after minor environmental stress (heat exposure). Symptoms progressed rapidly, featuring severe flushing, intense idiopathic urticaria spanning the trunk and limbs, and marked dermatographism where even clothing seams trigger new wheals. The patient denies shortness of breath, throat tightness, or gastrointestinal vomiting.
Functional Status: Ambulating but intensely distressed, pacing and continuously scratching.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high anxiety regarding potential airway closure if the reaction spreads internally.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Visibly flushed, diaphoretic, agitated due to severe pruritus. No stridor or wheeze.
Physical Exam: Airway patent; uvula midline, no posterior pharyngeal edema. Lungs clear to auscultation bilaterally. Dermatological exam reveals confluent, raised, erythematous urticarial plaques across 60 percent of body surface area. Stroking the unaffected skin produces immediate, pronounced linear wheals (positive dermatographism). Classic presentation of dermatological histamine hypersensitivity without systemic hypoperfusion.
Environmental Observations: House is very warm; advised immediate cooling.
Medication Reconciliation: Took one over-the-counter cetirizine 10mg one hour ago with zero clinical effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: MCAS Cutaneous Predominant - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of hypotension, no respiratory compromise (stridor/wheeze), and normal gastrointestinal function.

Acute viral exanthem - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Extreme rapidity of onset, intensely pruritic urticarial nature, and presence of dermatographism.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Widespread, bilateral, shifting nature of the wheals and absence of fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The explosive onset of widespread urticaria and flushing inherently carries a risk of progression to anaphylaxis. An urgent home visit was imperative to physically assess the airway, lung fields, and hemodynamics. By confirming a purely cutaneous mast cell degranulation event rather than systemic shock, emergency ambulance transport was avoided. However, the severity of the pruritus and failure of oral OTC antihistamines required immediate administration of potent parenteral agents to chemically stabilize the mast cells and relieve profound distress in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Promethazine 25mg IM to rapidly block H1 receptors and provide mild sedation to break the panic/scratching cycle. Prescribed Famotidine 20mg twice daily to block H2 receptors. Targeted intervention strategy includes Low Dose Naltrexone and Vitamin C.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking a cool (not cold) shower for 10 minutes when the itching spikes, you will physically constrict the superficial blood vessels, directly preventing further histamine release into the skin and rewarding yourself with immediate soothing relief.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Ice packs recommended for localized severe welts.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to optimize daily H1/H2 blocker regimen and consider mast cell stabilizers.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if patient develops a hoarse voice, difficulty swallowing, wheezing, or feelings of faintness/dizziness when standing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Cutaneous mast cell flares are unpredictable; while tonight's reaction is confined to the skin, future reactions require careful monitoring.
Clinical Course: The intense flushing and hives should significantly fade within 2-4 hours post-injection. The sedating antihistamine will facilitate sleep tonight.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if your tongue swells, your voice changes, or you suddenly struggle to pull air into your lungs.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body's immune cells (mast cells) have inappropriately dumped massive amounts of histamine into your skin; the injection acts as a chemical plug to block that histamine from causing further swelling and itching.
Actionable Steps: Wear loose, soft cotton clothing exclusively tomorrow; removing friction completely prevents the physical triggering of new hives, positively reinforcing the medication's calming effect on your skin.

